{
  "question": "What has been proposed to reduce the risk that an agent chooses unsuitable actions because different physical environments look identical to it, and by what mechanism?",
  "reference_answer": "One proposal is to augment the agent’s state with the environment’s sampled parameters θ, giving s′=concat(s,θ). Changes in physics can change which actions are optimal, while an instantaneous observation of joint positions and velocities may not reveal those changes. Appending θ removes this ambiguity: the different environments’ transition rules can be represented by one transition function over the augmented state. The policy can inspect θ and select the appropriate optimal subpolicy, making it possible for a single policy to be optimal across the environments. The document describes direct access to θ as a conceptual but impractical option; it establishes that an optimal policy can exist, rather than guaranteeing that training will find it.",
  "spans": [
    "In many MuJoCo tasks, the observation s contains only the positions and velocities of each robot joint, and without further modification it is not possible to recover θ, since changes in acceleration are not observable from velocity at a single time t. Therefore, a feedforward policy cannot simultaneously produce the optimal action a for both low gravity and high gravity environments, because the generalization benchmark has turned the MDP into a POMDP where θ is not observed, and the optimal actions a are genuinely different.",
    "Recalling that we would like there to be a single π∗ that is optimal across all MDPs at once, a conceptual but impractical option is to augment the state-space with the sampled parameter θ itself, with s′=concat(s,θ).",
    "This unifies all transitions Tθ into a single transition Tuniversal operating on this new space (i.e. the transition function can simply look at θ and pick which sub-transition to use). The optimality condition is satisfied because a policy can look at θ and pick the optimal subpolicy."
  ]
